WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.520
You know Boba Fett as an original&nbsp;
"Star Wars" trilogy villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:09.120
but did you know he could actually get worse?&nbsp;
From planting bombs to almost killing a baby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.160
these are the worst things&nbsp;
Boba Fett has ever done.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.040
In his first canonical appearance after&nbsp;
"Attack of the Clones," Boba Fett pursued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.800
a quest of vengeance against Mace Windu, the Jedi&nbsp;
responsible for beheading his father, Jango Fett.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.920
This plot unfolded in the second season of "Star&nbsp;
Wars: The Clone Wars" during the episode "Death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.800
Trap." In the episode, a young Fett takes on&nbsp;
the identity of a clone trooper named Lucky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.400
With this disguise, he's able to infiltrate Mace&nbsp;
Windu's quarters and place a motion-activated bomb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.600
in his room, all in the hopes of murdering the&nbsp;
Jedi and finally getting revenge for his dad.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.280
While Fett is successful in both&nbsp;
planting and setting off the bomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.480
it doesn't end up killing his intended target.&nbsp;
An innocent clone trooper is instead slaughtered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.960
after inadvertently setting the bomb off. But&nbsp;
in the wake of this unintentional killing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.520
Fett doesn't give up on his plans for&nbsp;
vengeance. Though he's initially reluctant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.080
he decides to attack the reactor core of&nbsp;
a Republic Star Destroyer. If it works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.000
it's a plan that wouldn't just kill&nbsp;
Windu, but also everyone else on board.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Through his bombing plan and subsequent&nbsp;
unsuccessful attack on the reactor core,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.800
it's clear that Fett is seriously grappling with&nbsp;
the loss of his father by inflicting more pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.360
on others. And it's also apparent that he's&nbsp;
more than willing to kill anyone who gets in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.920
the way of his target. He may still be a kid,&nbsp;
but Fett's already brutal beyond his years.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.120
A few seasons after his first appearance in "The&nbsp;
Clone Wars," Fett returned, a little older but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:38.640
still just as dangerous. In season 4's "Bounty,"&nbsp;
Fett had become an even more hardened soul thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.680
to an extended prison sentence. No longer&nbsp;
just seeking revenge for his father, Fett was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.040
inching close to his portrayal in "The Empire&nbsp;
Strikes Back" by making a living as a bounty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.520
hunter. To pursue this goal, Fett partnered&nbsp;
with a squad of fellow hunters, got a mask to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.280
conceal his identity, and took a job delivering&nbsp;
a large chest to the powerful ruler Otua Blank.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:04.160
While on his delivery mission, it was revealed&nbsp;
that the chest didn't contain jewels or weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:09.920
but instead held a young woman named Pluma Sodi&nbsp;
who was being sent to Blank as a forced bride.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.720
When Dark Side Force user Asajj Ventress, who'd&nbsp;
briefly joined Fett's crew for this assignment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:20.240
realized what their true cargo was, she hesitated.&nbsp;
But while the former Sith apprentice had a crisis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.960
of conscience, Fett demonstrated his growing&nbsp;
darkness by remaining firmly committed to the task&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.080
at hand. In Fett's previous "Clone Wars" story,&nbsp;
he seemed uncertain about hurting innocent people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.680
but this time Pluma Sodi's suffering fell on deaf&nbsp;
ears. Fett's ruthlessness sometimes manifests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.400
in shooting adversaries, and other times, it&nbsp;
emerges in ignoring the cries of the helpless.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:45.040
While it may seem like "The Empire Strikes&nbsp;
Back" was the first instance of Boba Fett&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.360
and Darth Vader crossing paths, media outside&nbsp;
of the movies has made it apparent that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:55.200
two characters met before. One such encounter&nbsp;
came in Marvel's 2015 "Star Wars" comic series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.000
which saw Boba Fett being hired by Darth Vader&nbsp;
to track down a figure who'd worked alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.240
Obi-Wan Kenobi during the events of "A&nbsp;
New Hope." Now on the hunt for his bounty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.040
Fett was willing to achieve his goal by&nbsp;
any means necessary ... including murder.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.240
While trying to track down his target,&nbsp;
Fett encountered a number of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.120
including Rodians and a group of moisture&nbsp;
farmers that he interrogated for information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.120
Once they told him what he needed to know, they&nbsp;
weren't released back to their humdrum lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.560
but instead were promptly killed by Fett. This&nbsp;
approach even extended to an unnamed farmer at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.920
the Mos Eisley Cantina who, after being viciously&nbsp;
interrogated, dropped a bombshell revelation to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.880
Fett that the man he was looking for was named&nbsp;
Luke Skywalker. In one of his earliest missions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:41.520
for Darth Vader, Fett was on a hot pursuit&nbsp;
that left a long trail of corpses behind.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.160
While many were introduced to Boba Fett&nbsp;
through "The Empire Strikes Back," it wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.240
the first appearance of the character.&nbsp;
Fett actually made his debut in one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:55.680
the most infamous pieces of "Star Wars"&nbsp;
media – "The Star Wars Holiday Special."

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.480
In between scenes featuring Harvey Korman as&nbsp;
a cooking show host and Bea Arthur crooning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.480
there was an animated segment entitled&nbsp;
"The Story of the Faithful Wookiee."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.640
In this short story, Boba Fett arrives&nbsp;
as a newly introduced character riding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.360
a dinosaur-esque creature. He seems to want&nbsp;
to help Luke Skywalker and friends on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:17.600
mission to retrieve a mystical object&nbsp;
that serves as the plot's MacGuffin.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.920
Though Fett initially acts as a helpful&nbsp;
figure, it's later revealed that he's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.800
ally. R2-D2 ends up discovering that Fett is&nbsp;
communicating with Darth Vader so that he can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.080
reveal the location of the Rebels to the villain.&nbsp;
This deception doesn't last long. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.920
R2 communicates this news to the rest of&nbsp;
the crew, which prompts anger from Han&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.880
Solo and forces Boba Fett to scurry away&nbsp;
— but not before vowing vengeance on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.880
team. The animated adventure doesn't&nbsp;
show us Boba Fett at his most grisly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.360
but it does show him as a duplicitous,&nbsp;
self-serving being right from the start.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:51.760
In "The Empire Strikes Back," Darth Vader&nbsp;
is a man on a mission. He's been chasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:56.000
after Luke Skywalker and company for quite&nbsp;
a while, but the good guys keep dodging him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.640
So the villain decides to hire a little extra&nbsp;
help, sending out a call for a crew of bounty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:05.600
hunters. Among those answering the call are&nbsp;
ruffians like Dengar, Bossk, and of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.880
Boba Fett in his first live-action appearance.&nbsp;
He isn't even on screen for a full minute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.280
before Vader turns to Fett and orders him&nbsp;
to refrain from disintegrating his quarry.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:19.280
Fett lives up to this reputation throughout&nbsp;
the rest of the movie as he slickly tracks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.320
an unwitting Han Solo through space. The bounty&nbsp;
hunter doesn't even blink at his mission here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:29.120
His only moment of real hesitation comes when&nbsp;
he thinks Solo might be killed in the carbonite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:34.000
freezing process. This isn't because he's worried&nbsp;
the process is too cruel. Instead, his only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:39.200
concern is how it'll impact his finances, since&nbsp;
Jabba the Hutt would like Solo to arrive alive.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.800
Fett's inclination to put money over&nbsp;
people and his ability to keep a cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.480
head are both exemplified throughout his&nbsp;
mission in "The Empire Strikes Back."

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:51.360
Everyone loves Grogu. That little guy&nbsp;
formerly known to fans as Baby Yoda has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.720
quickly become one of the most beloved&nbsp;
"Star Wars" characters of all time.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:56.240
"Stop touching things"

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:09.360
In the process of spawning memes all over the&nbsp;
internet, he's also helped put "The Mandalorian"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:15.360
on the map. And only 14 episodes into the show's&nbsp;
run, audiences had become totally captivated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.560
Grogu ... which made him the perfect target to&nbsp;
emphasize the cold-hearted nature of Boba Fett.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:22.560
When Fett first meets Din Djarin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:26.960
the lead character of "The Mandalorian,"&nbsp;
he's not there purely to shed more blood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.200
Fett, having emerged from the Sarlacc pit,&nbsp;
wants to get his Mandalorian armor back.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.000
"I don't want your armor. I want my armor."

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.920
To instill some leverage into the scenario,&nbsp;
Fett remarks that he's got an ally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.520
Fennec Shand, poised with a sniper rifle nearby.&nbsp;
While Djarin notes that his armor would protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:50.320
him from any sniper blast, Fett clarifies&nbsp;
that Djarin wouldn't be Shand's target.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.280
Instead, at Fett's signal, the&nbsp;
sniper would shoot and kill Grogu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:57.680
who's busy meditating so that he can call&nbsp;
out for any other Jedi in the galaxy.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.640
By the time this episode aired, Boba Fett had&nbsp;
existed in pop culture for over four decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:07.120
and his ruthless reputation was well-known. But&nbsp;
by threatening to kill a beloved character like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.960
Grogu, Fett's cold-hearted nature&nbsp;
was taken to a whole other level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.680
and established for a whole new&nbsp;
generation of "Star Wars" fans.

